  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Published in 2024 by Peter Lang Verlag, this edited collection on educational agency and activism in linguistic landscape studies has accumulated 345 downloads and seven scholarly citations within its first two years, indicating emerging adoption in a specialized research community. The work is available in open access through OAPEN and Peter Lang's platform, and has received formal peer review through PRISM. Early citing works span Europe\u2014from Germany and Romania to Greece, Norway, and Sweden\u2014and cluster around themes of multilingual education policy, linguistic ideology, and semiotic activism in educational spaces.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Edited by Durk Gorter and Edina Kromp\u00e1k, this book explores how agency and activism shape linguistic landscapes in educational settings\u2014a theme that integrates linguistics, geography, and educational sociology. The volume expands the established field of educationscapes research by bringing together international contributions on linguistic, semiotic, and spatial agency across diverse educational contexts. Its open-access status (gold OA) and placement with a major academic publisher position it for sustained reach within scholarly and educational communities.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">Early evidence of scholarly uptake appears solid for a 2024 publication. Seven citing works have already referenced the volume across international journals including Emotion, Space and Society, the International Journal of Adolescence and Youth, and the field-specific Linguistic Landscape journal. Five of the seven citing works engage multilingual education and policy\u2014the book's primary intellectual focus\u2014while others extend its framework to unexpected domains: one application examines linguistic ideology in Spain, another analyzes semiotic signs in German school communication systems, and a third applies educationscape concepts to prison classrooms in Greece. This thematic diversity suggests the volume's framework resonates across applied linguistics, education studies, and social geography.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">Usage metrics reinforce this trajectory. The work has been downloaded 345 times via OAPEN, with peak activity in April (77 downloads) and a sustained pattern through mid-year. WorldCat records indicate institutional library holdings, though specific adoption in course curricula is not documented in available sources. A formal book review appeared in Linguistic Landscape (2025), authored by Enik\u0151 Bir\u00f3, who has also cited the work in related publications on multilingual university contexts. This reviewer engagement suggests the work has been vetted within the linguistic landscape research community and positioned as a reference point for ongoing scholarship.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">The citing landscape reveals concentration at six European institutions, particularly Universit\u00e4t Hamburg and Sapientia Hungarian University of Transylvania (two citations each), alongside individual citations from institutions in Italy, Greece, Norway, and Sweden. This geographic spread, combined with citations appearing in both 2025 and 2026, indicates sustained rather than momentary attention. The book's contribution to theorizing activism and agency in educational spaces appears to be establishing a foothold in European-centered research networks, particularly those focused on multilingualism and educational semiotics.</p>",
